  cites Mathbook chapter Simplify

 The browser interface for the notebook, and it is a different shape from the
 arcade's modules on purpose.

 A game exports i32 functions and is handed throwing WASI stubs, because its
 whole state fits in integers. A computer algebra system answers TEXT, and a
 Codex `Text` is a pointer to a four-byte length followed by CCE bytes, which
 a page would have to decode with a CCE table it does not have. The compiler's
 own page solved this years before this chapter existed: the module is a WASI
 program, the expression arrives on `fd_read` and the answer leaves on
 `fd_write`, and both are plain UTF-8 because the foreword converts at the I/O
 boundary rather than in the middle (R-CCE). So the page needs no codec, and
 what crosses the boundary is a line of text in and a line of text out.

 `_start` runs once and exits, so the page instantiates the module per
 evaluation. That is what the compile page does per compile, and it is also
 what makes the module safe to run without a collector: each evaluation gets a
 fresh heap rather than accumulating one.

Section: One Evaluation

 Parse, simplify, print. The simplifier's entry is `simplify`, which walks the
 children and iterates to a fixed point under `simplify-fuel`; `fold-constants`
 is one pass at the TOP node only and is the wrong door. The two agree on
 `2+2` and disagree on `1+2*3`, where the top-only pass sees an `ExInt` beside
 an unfolded `ExMul` and answers `1 + 2 * 3`. An operand pair that cannot tell
 the two apart is what makes a wrong entry ship, so the grader asks the nested
 question rather than the flat one.

  mb-eval : Text -> Text
  mb-eval (line) =
    let parsed = parse-expr line
    in if parsed.po-ok
       then ex-print (simplify (parsed.po-expr))
       else "parse error: " & parsed.po-error

Section: Entry Point

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    entered <- read-line
    when entered
      is Just (line) -> print-line-uni (mb-eval line)
      is otherwise -> print-line-uni "parse error: no input"
    end
  end

